AUDIO: 'A new study from Stanford Medicine is changing our understanding of the brain, as researchers previously believed it developed from a single cell. But now they claim that the brain is actually two'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'separate organs located side by side. This is fascinating. To explain more, we\'re joined by one of the researchers who led this study, Dr. Kyle Lowe. Dr. Kyle,'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'thank you for joining us. Hello, nice to be here. This is truly impressive. Yes. I think as a child, when you first start learning about the brain, they tell you about the right and left hemispheres'

[00:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '. And as you get older, you learn about the frontal lobe and other parts, but the fact that they are two separate organs is just amazing. This is something completely new'

[00:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that no one was aware of before. Thank you. Well, scientists have been working on the origins of the brain for a long time, and I should explain in a little more detail. As you know, we usually'

[01:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'think of the brain as a single organ. Yes. But in reality it consists of two very different parts. The front part of the brain is responsible for creative thinking, memory—all the things that I hope you\'re using right now . And the back part,'

[01:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'called the hindbrain , controls vital functions. These are processes such as nutrition, sleep, wakefulness, and even consciousness. We discovered that the brain is not a single organ. When a fetus develops in'

[01:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the womb, it actually forms from two different sources—an anterior and posterior part that fuse together to form what appears to be a single organ. You can draw an analogy with a smartphone, which is assembled from many'

[01:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'different parts in different countries. But in the end they are combined into one complete device. And that\'s where you started, right? You mentioned that the discovery came while studying'

[01:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'fetal development. Embryonic development. That\'s right. We studied how the brain is formed when a person develops in the womb. One part that particularly surprised us was the implications for'

[02:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'understanding how the brain evolved. After all, as you know, the question of the origin of the brain is one of the biggest in science. I\'ll be happy to talk about this or anything else I can. So, let\'s dig into this,'

[02:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'because, you know, evolutionarily and even in modern animals like chickens, squids, or jellyfish, right? They have a separate brain and a separate nervous system, and you\'re saying we have something similar,'

[02:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'but ours just fused together in the skull? That\'s right. So, we think that the idea of the brain as a single organ may be somewhat of a historical accident, because scientists and doctors have'

[02:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'always believed that we have one brain. But if you look at jellyfish that live in the ocean, they don\'t have one brain, but actually two physically separate nerve rings at different ends of their bodies. Therefore, we believe that evolution'

[02:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'may not have had a grand plan to create a single brain, but rather that jellyfish had a plan to create two separate nervous systems in different parts of the body. Then, when it came to us, we didn\'t invent anything new'

[03:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'by creating a common founder cell for the brain, but rather took those two ancient nerve rings and connected them to get something that looks like our brain. So we find it really amazing because we diverged from jellyfish over 500'

